  "title": "Bargaining at the Maasai Market",
  "category": "commerce",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "level": 6,
  "order": 604,
  "theme": "market",
  "culturalNote": "The Maasai Market moves around Nairobi through the week — rows of baskets (vikapu), beaded jewellery, kikoi cloths and carvings. The first price is for tourists; bargaining is expected and friendly. The rhythm: show interest, compare (\"this one is nicer than that one\"), name a price, and settle with \"Tumepatana!\" (we've agreed). The -me- tense is everywhere in a deal: \"nimeona\" (I've seen), \"umepunguza\" (you've lowered it), \"nimeamua\" (I've decided).",
  "startingPoint": "Saturday afternoon at the Maasai Market. A seller waves you over to her baskets.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "perfect-me",
    "perfect-negative-ja",
    "comparatives",
    "adjective-agreement"
  ],
